BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-new-york-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: New York Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's New York Fellowship puts a cross-d
 isciplinary cohort of artists\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia 
 in the United States for six months to live with a new cultural landscape:
  attending performances\, exhibitions\, workshops and discussions\, and bu
 ilding relationships with peers and with ACC's alumni network. It is delib
 erately an extended pause from daily work with NO obligation to produce or
  present anything. Applicants must be citizens or legal permanent resident
 s of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: Afghanistan\, Banglade
 sh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kon
 g SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, 
 Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lank
 a\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): a
 rchitecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeology\, art history\, ar
 ts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\, da
 nce\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, theat
 er and visual art\; video art and photography apply under visual art. Addi
 tional requirements: at least 21 years old by the start of the fellowship\
 , at least five years of professional experience in an eligible discipline
 \, no paid work\, university coursework or teaching (online included) duri
 ng the fellowship\, and enough confident English to handle daily life inde
 pendently\, as the J-1 visa requires. Previous New York Fellows cannot rea
 pply\, and anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years
  is ineligible. VISA: fellows must enter the U.S. on an ACC-sponsored J-1 
 visa with no exceptions\, which bars paid work of any kind during the fell
 owship\, including work for a non-U.S. employer and participation in ticke
 ted events even unpaid. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: New York City a
 nd the wider United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents
  of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citize
 ns and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 38\,000 for 
 the six-month fellowship\, including a USD 3\,000 advance for travel costs
 . ACC deducts certain pre-arrival costs from the grant: the USD 220 SEVIS 
 fee\, mandatory travel health insurance (roughly USD 675 to 800) and Inter
 national House housing if you take it (roughly USD 19\,400 to 20\,600 for 
 six months). Fellows can expect USD 10\,000 to 31\,100 in hand after arriv
 al depending on insurance and housing choices. Also included: ACC-organise
 d group activities and institutional visits\, individual programmatic and 
 logistical support\, and entry to the ACC alumni network. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Six months\, in one of two cohorts: 1 January to 30 June 20
 28\, or 1 July to 31 December 2028. ACC assigns the cohort.\n\nMore: https
 ://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City and the wider United States. Open to citizens and le
 gal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and region
 s in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jentel-artist-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Jentel Artist Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Jentel Artist Residency offers approximately three-week res
 idencies in the foothills of the Bighorn Mountains near Banner\, Wyoming\,
  giving artists\, writers\, and composers dedicated time for creative work
 . Sessions run from mid-January through early December\, hosting six resid
 ents at a time. It is open to visual artists in any medium\, writers of fi
 ction\, creative nonfiction\, or poetry\, and composers across musical gen
 res. Residents receive a private bedroom\, a separate studio\, shared livi
 ng and kitchen space\, and a USD 100 weekly stipend to help cover food and
  personal expenses. Applicants must be at least 25 years old and be U.S. c
 itizens or foreign nationals whose primary residence is in the U.S. during
  the application year\, and may not be enrolled full-time in a degree prog
 ram. There is a USD 30 non-refundable application fee\, with deadlines of 
 September 15 for Winter and Spring sessions and January 15 for Summer and 
 Fall sessions.\n\nOrganisation: Jentel Foundation\nLocation: Banner\, Wyom
 ing\, USA\nAward: USD 100 weekly stipend (about USD 300 total over three w
 eeks)\, plus private bedroom\, separate studio space\, and shared living a
 nd kitchen facilities provided at no cost USD 30 application fee\nDuration
 : Approximately 3 weeks (arrive on the 15th\, depart the morning of the 7t
 h of the following month)\n\nMore: https://www.jentelarts.org/apply?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.jentelarts.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Banner\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:simons-foundation-open-interval-turbulence-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Interval: An Artist-Scientist Exploration of Turbule
 nce
DESCRIPTION:Open Interval is a Simons Foundation program that supports trio
 s consisting of an artist\, a scientist\, and a representative from a 501(
 c)(3) arts organization to collaborate around a shared scientific theme. T
 he current cycle centers on the theme of turbulence\, a phenomenon at the 
 edge of chaos and beauty. Awarded trios receive USD 30\,000 to support a s
 ustained period of thought partnership and exploration rather than the pro
 duction of finished works or exhibitions. The program runs from April to D
 ecember 2027 with a touchstone meeting in May 2027 and possible cohort con
 venings. Trio members must be regionally co-located anywhere in the United
  States so they can collaborate in person. The scientist should hold a Ph.
 D. and be conducting basic research\, with priority given to fields such a
 s astrophysics\, neuroscience\, quantum physics\, biology\, or mathematics
 . The application portal opens September 9\, 2026\, and all three trio mem
 bers must submit for the application to be complete.\n\nOrganisation: Simo
 ns Foundation\nLocation: United States (trio must be regionally co-located
 )\nAward: USD 30\,000 per awarded trio\nDuration: April to December 2027 (
 several months of collaboration)\n\nMore: https://www.simonsfoundation.org
 /2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-an-artist-scientist-exploration-of-t
 urbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-a
 n-artist-scientist-exploration-of-turbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (trio must be regionally co-located)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:helene-wurlitzer-foundation-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270118
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270119
SUMMARY:Deadline: Helene Wurlitzer Foundation Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Helene Wurlitzer Foundation of New Mexico runs one of the o
 ldest artist residency programs in the United States\, established in 1954
 \, on a 15-acre campus in Taos\, New Mexico. The residency is open to visu
 al artists\, literary artists\, and music composers\, including painters\,
  sculptors\, photographers\, filmmakers\, writers\, poets\, playwrights\, 
 screenwriters\, and songwriters. Selected grantees receive rent-free and u
 tility-paid housing in a furnished casita for a session of 10 to 12 weeks\
 , with access to a Commons House offering WiFi\, a library\, and bicycles.
  No stipend is provided in most cases\, and residents cover their own food
  and transportation. Applications are open to creative artists worldwide r
 egardless of age\, experience\, or nationality\, and the annual deadline i
 s January 18 for residencies the following calendar year. There is a non-w
 aivable USD 30 application fee.\n\nOrganisation: The Helene Wurlitzer Foun
 dation of New Mexico\nLocation: Taos\, New Mexico\, USA\nAward: Rent-free\
 , utility-paid furnished housing (casita)\; no stipend (rare stipend suppo
 rt via the Rhonda Artist Stipend Fund)\; artists cover food and travel USD
  30 application fee\nDuration: 10 to 12 weeks (Sessions 1 and 3 are 12 wee
 ks\, Session 2 is 10 weeks)\n\nMore: https://wurlitzerfoundation.org/apply
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://wurlitzerfoundation.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Taos\, New Mexico\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:grand-canyon-conservancy-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Grand Canyon Conservancy Residency 2027 (Artist / Astrono
 mer / Environmental Educator)
DESCRIPTION:Grand Canyon Conservancy's award-winning residency program invi
 tes artists\, scientists\, historians and educators to live on site at Gra
 nd Canyon National Park\, pursue place-based research\, and engage the pub
 lic through meaningful programs that deepen understanding of the park's en
 vironmental\, spiritual and cultural impact. Founded in 2020\; competitive
  application + peer-panel review. THREE TRACKS: (1) Artist in Residence - 
 for contemporary artists making interactive (immersive and/or participator
 y) work that shapes how people experience place\; (2) Astronomer in Reside
 nce - for astronomers from any discipline who wish to share their knowledg
 e and enthusiasm for dark skies\; (3) Environmental Educator in Residence 
 - for educators who use unique and inspiring methods to pique curiosity\, 
 prompt exploration\, and build knowledge of the world. Residents receive f
 ree accommodation\, financial support\, and marketing/exposure\; alumni re
 turn as part of a nationwide network. Read the individual track pages (lin
 ked from the main residency page) for track-specific eligibility\, session
  lengths\, stipend amounts\, public-engagement expectations and any region
 /citizenship requirements before applying. APPLICATIONS OPEN: 15 May - 15 
 July 2026 for the 2027 cycle.\n\nOrganisation: Grand Canyon Conservancy (G
 rand Canyon National Park)\nLocation: Grand Canyon National Park\, Arizona
 \, United States\nAward: Free on-site accommodation in the park\, financia
 l support (honorarium/stipend\; amount varies by track and session - confi
 rm on the track-specific page)\, plus marketing/exposure through the Conse
 rvancy and the National Park. Alumni network access. No application fee. T
 hree tracks (Artist / Astronomer / Environmental Educator) - apply to one 
 track per cycle.\nDuration: Place-based residency at Grand Canyon National
  Park during 2027\; dates and length vary by track\; residents are expecte
 d to engage the public through programs\, workshops\, talks or similar\n\n
 More: https://www.grandcanyon.org/experience-grand-canyon/residency-progra
 m?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.grandcanyon.org/experience-grand-canyon/residency-program?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Grand Canyon National Park\, Arizona\, United States
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fulbright-imera-mediterranean-studies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fulbright-Iméra Mediterranean Studies Award 2027-2028 (A
 ix-Marseille\, US Faculty)
DESCRIPTION:The Fulbright-Iméra Mediterranean Studies Award supports US fa
 culty to conduct research at the Iméra Institute for Advanced Study at Ai
 x Marseille Université. ELIGIBILITY: mid-career\, senior\, or community c
 ollege faculty affiliated with a US institution of higher education\; US c
 itizens residing outside France (permanent residents of France are ineligi
 ble)\; any discipline relating to Mediterranean studies\; English sufficie
 nt (French desirable). 1 or 2 grants awarded. PROVIDED: €2\,400/month st
 ipend\; fully equipped apartment with linens\; office in co-working space\
 ; visa fees waived\; academic event organisation expenses covered\; school
  placement assistance for dependents. DURATION: 5 or 10 months (September 
 2027 – June 2028\, flexible). DEADLINE: 15 September 2026. Apply via the
  Fulbright Scholar Program at https://fulbrightscholars.org/award/fulbrigh
 t-imera-mediterranean-studies-award-aix-marseille-university.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Iméra – Aix Marseille Université\, in partnership with the Fulbri
 ght Commission\nLocation: Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)\nAward
 : €2\,400 (~USD 2\,600) monthly stipend\; fully covered accommodation (v
 alued USD 788–1\,966/month)\; €1\,200 (~USD 1\,300) travel allowance. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: 5 or 10 months (September 2027 – June 202
 8\, flexible start/end dates).\n\nMore: https://fulbrightscholars.org/awar
 d/fulbright-imera-mediterranean-studies-award-aix-marseille-university?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fulbrightscholars.org/award/fulbright-imera-mediterranean-studi
 es-award-aix-marseille-university?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-general-residency-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross General Residency (All Disciplines): Spring 2027
DESCRIPTION:Ucross offers fully funded artist residencies on a working catt
 le ranch in the foothills of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains\, providing unint
 errupted time and space free from the pressures and distractions of daily 
 life. Founded in 1981 with its first residency period in 1983\, Ucross hos
 ts approximately 115 individuals per year. Residencies range from two to s
 ix weeks\; at any one time up to ten people are in residence\, typically f
 our writers\, four visual artists\, and two composers. Each artist receive
 s living accommodations\, meals\, and work space at no charge. Lunches and
  dinners are prepared Monday to Friday by a professional chef (lunches del
 ivered to studios\, group dinners at 6 p.m.)\, with ample provisions for b
 reakfasts and weekends\; towels\, linens\, and weekly housekeeping are pro
 vided\, and there is cell service and wireless internet throughout. Reside
 nts provide their own working materials and arrange their own travel to Sh
 eridan\, Wyoming. The program is open to visual artists\, writers\, compos
 ers\, choreographers\, interdisciplinary artists\, performance artists\, a
 nd collaborative teams\; applicants must exhibit professional standing in 
 their field\, and both established and emerging artists are encouraged to 
 apply. Applications are submitted by discipline category: dance\, music/so
 und\, performance\, film/video\, playwriting/screenwriting\, visual art\, 
 fiction\, nonfiction\, poetry\, or interdisciplinary. The work sample and 
 project proposal are the most significant features of the application and 
 should correspond to one another\; if your work is in a single discipline\
 , select the category that best fits your project\, and apply under interd
 isciplinary only if the project genuinely combines multiple disciplines. W
 ork-sample requirements by category: DANCE\, 2-3 video files (each up to 8
  minutes and 200 MB\, or up to 3 Vimeo links\, with a timestamp if longer 
 than 8 minutes)\; MUSIC/SOUND\, 3-5 audio files (each up to 8 minutes and 
 200 MB\, or up to 3 Vimeo links\; musical scores welcome)\; PERFORMANCE an
 d FILM/VIDEO\, 1-3 video files (each up to 5 minutes and under 200 MB\, or
  up to 3 Vimeo/YouTube links\, with a timestamp if longer than 5 minutes)\
 ; PLAYWRITING/SCREENWRITING\, one complete play double-spaced noting the 2
 0-page section to read\; VISUAL ART\, 10 images at 4x6 inches and 300 dpi 
 (JPG or TIF\, no PDFs) plus a data sheet listing title\, medium\, size\, a
 nd year\; FICTION and NONFICTION\, 20 pages of double-spaced writing\; POE
 TRY\, 6-10 poems (10 maximum) in one PDF\, each poem starting on a new pag
 e\, total not exceeding 15 pages\; INTERDISCIPLINARY\, samples representin
 g the genres you plan to work in\, combining any of the above. All written
  work samples must be in English. There is a USD 40 application fee. The o
 pen call for the Spring 2027 session is open May 1 to July 15\, 2026 (appl
 y by 11:59 p.m. MT on July 15). Dedicated Fellowships for Native American 
 Visual Artists and Writers and a separate Culinary Residency are also offe
 red.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foundation\nLocation: Sheridan\, Wyoming\, US
 A\nAward: No charge for the residency\; living accommodations\, meals\, an
 d work space provided. USD 40 application fee.\nDuration: Spring session (
 February through early June 2027)\; residencies range from two to six week
 s\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloedel-reserve-creative-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative Residency at Bloedel Reserve: Class of 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bloedel Reserve's residency programme exists to foster creative
  thinking inspired by nature and to explore the connection between humans 
 and the environment. It is open to professional and emerging artists\, com
 posers\, writers\, botanists\, landscape architects\, researchers and othe
 rs working on themes of nature and human connection to it. Residents get a
  three-week stay at a 140-acre internationally recognised public garden an
 d forest preserve\, housed in a small home hidden in the woods off the mai
 n paths. Note for non-US applicants: the funding is structured as expense 
 reimbursement rather than a stipend\, capped at USD 1\,000\, which for tra
 nsatlantic applicants is unlikely to cover airfare plus meals over three w
 eeks.\n\nOrganisation: Bloedel Reserve\nLocation: Bainbridge Island\, Wash
 ington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kitsap Peninsula\, reachable by fer
 ry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap Peninsula. Residents must be 21 o
 r older. International applicants are eligible but are funded differently 
 from domestic ones (see amount).\nAward: USD 22 application fee. Furnished
  private house on the grounds with linens\, stocked kitchen\, wi-fi\, laun
 dry and use of a smart car\; an electric piano is available. Domestic resi
 dents receive a USD 1\,000 stipend. International residents receive no sti
 pend and may instead submit up to USD 1\,000 of expenses for reimbursement
 . Residents pay for their own meals and their own travel to Bainbridge Isl
 and. There is no dedicated studio\; residents work in the house.\nDuration
 : Three weeks\; residents stay one at a time with no overlap\n\nMore: http
 s://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
URL:https://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
LOCATION:Bainbridge Island\, Washington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kit
 sap Peninsula\, reachable by ferry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap P
 eninsula. Residents must be 21 or older. International applicants are elig
 ible but are funded differently from domestic ones (see amount).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mad-artist-studios-residency-cycle-46-47@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: MAD Artist Studios Residency\, Cycles 46 and 47
DESCRIPTION:Established in 2008 and the first of its kind\, the Artist Stud
 ios at MAD is a public-facing community engagement and professional develo
 pment programme on the sixth floor of the Museum\, where visitors meet art
 ists-in-residence at work. Two artists are selected per six-month session 
 (the application text elsewhere says three\, so confirm with MAD). More th
 an 200 artists and designers have come through the programme\, which MAD d
 escribes as an innovative model for museum education\, with a sustained fo
 cus on artists from historically underrepresented communities whose work p
 ushes the boundaries of contemporary craft\; alumni have exhibited at the 
 Queens Museum\, Whitney\, Brooklyn Museum and Studio Museum. WHAT MAD MEAN
 S BY CRAFT: both a discipline and a way of thinking\, covering traditional
  studio craft materials such as clay\, fiber\, wood\, glass\, metal and st
 one\, and craft-based methods used in new ways\, including handwork combin
 ed with digital tools\, performance or experimental materials. Craft here 
 is defined by deep engagement with process\, materiality and the hand rath
 er than by medium. RESIDENCY EXPECTATIONS: this is a substantial public co
 mmitment\, not quiet studio time. Residents commit to 14 or 21 public open
  studio hours a week on Thursdays\, Fridays and/or Saturdays 10:30am to 5:
 30pm and/or Wednesdays 11:00am to 7:00pm\, on a schedule fixed with MAD's 
 Education Department for the whole six months. During those hours resident
 s host K-12 school groups (in person and virtual) and public tour groups. 
 Because of the contact with schoolchildren\, all selected artists must pas
 s a background check. ELIGIBILITY: an artist or designer with an active st
 udio practice in the applied arts\; a resident of the New York City metro 
 area\, defined as the five boroughs plus nearby New York\, New Jersey and 
 Connecticut counties within reasonable commuting distance\, typically on M
 TA\, PATH or NJ Transit\; at least 21 at the time of application\; and a U
 S citizen or legal US resident with a valid SSN or EIN. The Museum does no
 t sponsor visas and states this is not subject to exception. Programme alu
 mni may not reapply\, though unsuccessful past applicants may. SELECTION: 
 the committee looks for a mature body of work engaging critically with the
  transdisciplinary fields of contemporary craft\, design\, art and technol
 ogy\, a strong conceptual framework\, deep engagement with process and mat
 erials\, a clear direction or question for the residency\, and genuine rea
 diness for the open studios format. Top applications go to interview with 
 the Deputy Director of Education and Manager of Public Programs and Studio
 s\, and finalists' references are contacted. Applicants are strongly encou
 raged to visit the studios in person first. NOT AN EXHIBITION PROGRAMME: r
 esidents may show work in progress in their studio and finished work in th
 e sixth-floor vitrines\, but the residency does not include a gallery exhi
 bition or a proposal for MAD's galleries. STUDIO RESTRICTIONS: no open fla
 mes\, all hot work approved by the Director of Facilities\, no large or un
 safe power tools such as saws\, and no toxic materials including turpentin
